WEBVTT

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.799
Rural health care isn't simply
city health care with fewer people.

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:08.348
In this section, we'll look at
five differences that matter,

00:00:08.348 --> 00:00:10.610
and what each one means for AI.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.436
By one common count,
about 56 million Americans,

00:00:14.436 --> 00:00:17.135
nearly one in five, live in rural areas.

00:00:17.135 --> 00:00:21.546
On average, rural communities have
higher death rates from trauma,

00:00:21.546 --> 00:00:24.250
stroke, and cancer than cities do.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.546
First, fewer clinicians and longer trips.

00:00:27.546 --> 00:00:31.525
Rural areas are short of both
family doctors and specialists.

00:00:31.525 --> 00:00:36.724
And rural Americans live, on average,
about ten miles from the nearest hospital,

00:00:36.724 --> 00:00:39.820
compared with about four miles in cities.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.744
Second, hospitals under pressure.

00:00:42.744 --> 00:00:46.231
Since 2005, nearly 200
rural hospitals have closed

00:00:46.231 --> 00:00:48.691
or stopped offering inpatient care.

00:00:48.691 --> 00:00:54.150
Each closure can mean longer trips in
an emergency, and fewer local jobs.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.618
Third, little technical help.

00:00:56.618 --> 00:01:00.292
A small rural hospital may not
have a dedicated IT leader.

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:04.464
And rural hospitals rely more
heavily than urban ones on the tools

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:08.600
that come built into their
electronic health record system.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.692
Fourth, weaker digital infrastructure.

00:01:11.692 --> 00:01:15.333
Many rural areas still lag
behind in broadband internet.

00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:19.422
A tool that needs a fast connection
to the cloud can slow down,

00:01:19.422 --> 00:01:22.250
or stop working, at the worst moment.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.231
Fifth, small numbers.

00:01:24.231 --> 00:01:28.878
A rural clinic may see only a handful
of patients with a given condition.

00:01:28.878 --> 00:01:32.143
That makes it hard to build
a tool from local data,

00:01:32.143 --> 00:01:36.790
or to check whether a tool works there.
In a review of rural AI research,

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.020
half the studies named a lack
of reliable data as a problem.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:45.376
Rural communities also have real
strengths: close relationships,

00:01:45.376 --> 00:01:48.669
local knowledge,
and staff who know their patients well.

00:01:48.669 --> 00:01:52.940
Good technology should build on
those strengths, not replace them.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.479
Put together,
a tool that works well in a large

00:01:56.479 --> 00:01:59.417
city hospital may not
fit a small rural clinic.

00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:02.111
It may need fast internet,
technical staff,

00:02:02.111 --> 00:02:04.960
or data the clinic simply doesn't have.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:10.238
So before asking whether an AI tool is
good, ask whether it fits the place.

00:02:10.238 --> 00:02:14.270
Next, we'll look at where AI
in rural health stands today.
